 **Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-*   **Precision:** Using `RFloat` (32-bit) is critical for distance accuracy. Lower precision formats will cause artifacts in Eikonal propagation [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Boundary Conditions:** The `neighborMin` function accesses neighbors. The source assumes full access, implying the kernel size and dispatch cover the volume without out-of-bounds reads, or the texture handles wrapping/clamping [G].
-*   **Eikonal Validity:** The Eikonal solution is only valid if the gradient magnitude is 1. The `eikonal` function approximates this. If `d < 0` in `eikonal3d`, it falls back to 2D solutions. This ensures stability but may reduce accuracy in complex topologies [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Brush Size vs. Resolution:** If `BrushSize` is smaller than the voxel size, the brush may not be visible. The source scales the brush size for subtraction (`* 1.25f`) to compensate [S1, unofficial].
+- **Precision:** The use of `RFloat` (32-bit float) may lead to precision issues for very large volumes or fine details. [G]
+- **Boundary Conditions:** The `neighborMin` function accesses neighbors. At volume boundaries, these accesses may read uninitialized or zero values, potentially causing artifacts. [G]
+- **Bitwise Operation:** The source uses `outSdfTex = (_activeSdfTex + 1) & 2`. For a two-buffer system, this should likely be `& 1`. If `& 2` is intentional, it implies a different indexing scheme or a bug. [S1, unofficial]
+- **Eikonal Passes Calculation:** The formula `_eikonelPasses = (int)(BrushSize / (SDFRadius * 2.0 / SDFResolution.x)) * 2` assumes a specific relationship between brush size, SDF radius, and resolution. Incorrect parameters may lead to insufficient propagation. [S1, unofficial]
 
 ## Subtopics
 
 ### [SDF Mutation & Eikonal Propagation](4752-sdf-mutation-eikonal-propagation.md)
-SDF Mutation & Eikonal Propagation is a GPU-based method for interactively modifying Signed Distance Fields (SDFs) using volumetric brushes. It solves the problem of maintaining geometrically correct distance values after local modifications (addition or subtraction) by combining immediate ray-casting-based brush application with iterative Eikonal equation propagation. It differs from static SDF generation by supporting real-time user input and from simple voxel carving by ensuring the resulting field remains a valid distance function through distance propagation.
+SDF Mutation & Eikonal Propagation is a GPU-based method for interactively modifying Signed Distance Fields (SDFs) using volumetric brushes. It solves the problem of maintaining geometrically correct distance values after local modifications (addition or subtraction) by combining immediate ray-casting-based brush application with iterative Eikonal equation propagation. It differs from static SDF generation by supporting real-time user input and from simple voxel carving by ensuring the resulting field remains a valid distance function through distance propagation [G].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N)$ per voxel per pass, where $N$ is the number of voxels in the volume. The number of passes depends on the brush size and resolution [G].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $2 \times \text{SDFResolution.x} \times \text{SDFResolution.y} \times \text{SDFResolution.z} \times 4$ bytes (for two `RFloat` textures) [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Performance:** The method is designed for real-time interaction on the GPU. Performance scales with resolution and the number of Eikonal passes [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Real-time interactive modification, geometrically valid SDFs, efficient GPU implementation [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Limited to GPU environments, requires careful tuning of brush size and resolution, Eikonal propagation is an approximation [S1, unofficial].
-*   **When to Use:** Interactive SDF editing, real-time procedural geometry generation [S1, unofficial].
-*   **When Not to Use:** Static SDF generation, CPU-only environments, high-precision offline rendering [G].
+- **Time Complexity:** O(N) per voxel per pass, where N is the number of voxels in the volume. Eikonal propagation requires multiple passes, scaling with the brush radius and resolution.
+- **Memory Complexity:** O(N) for the two ping-pong buffers.
+- **Measured Performance:** Not given in the sources.
+- **Scaling:** Performance degrades with increased resolution and brush size due to more Eikonal passes.
+- **Strengths:** Real-time interactive modification, GPU-accelerated, maintains distance field validity.
+- **Limitations:** Limited to GPU execution, potential precision issues, boundary artifacts.
+- **When to use:** Interactive SDF editing, real-time volume modification.
+- **When not to use:** Static SDF generation, CPU-only environments, high-precision requirements.
 
 ## Background
-This method is derived from the implementation in the Unity project `samuelbigos/signed_distance_fields_in_unity` [S1, unofficial]. It leverages GPU compute shaders for parallel processing and ping-pong buffering to handle read/write conflicts [S1, unofficial]. The Eikonal propagation is based on the fast marching method or similar distance transform algorithms adapted for GPU execution [G].
+This method originates from the Unity-based implementation by samuelbigos, as documented in the GitHub repository `samuelbigos/signed_distance_fields_in_unity` [S1, unofficial]. It is designed for interactive SDF manipulation within the Unity engine, leveraging compute shaders for parallel processing.
 
